### What is a queue?

A queue is a linear data structure that follows First In First Out ordering. Elements are added at one end called the rear and removed from the other end called the front. The element that has been waiting the longest is always the next one served. This makes a queue the natural choice whenever fairness and ordering matter.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Queue;

public class TransferQueue {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Queue<String> pending = new ArrayDeque<>();
        pending.offer("TXN-1001: Rajesh transfer 50000");
        pending.offer("TXN-1002: Priya transfer 25000");
        pending.offer("TXN-1003: Pankaj transfer 75000");

        System.out.println("Front: " + pending.peek());
        System.out.println("Served: " + pending.poll());
        System.out.println("Next: " + pending.peek());
    }
}
```

```text
Front: TXN-1001: Rajesh transfer 50000
Served: TXN-1001: Rajesh transfer 50000
Next: TXN-1002: Priya transfer 25000
```

### Why do we need a queue?

A queue exists because many real systems need to process items in the order they arrive. A bank cannot serve the customer who walked in last while someone who arrived an hour ago is still waiting. A message broker cannot deliver message number 5 before message number 1. FIFO ordering guarantees fairness, and a queue enforces it structurally.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Queue;

public class LoanApplicationQueue {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Queue<String> applications = new ArrayDeque<>();
        applications.offer("LOAN-2001: Rajesh home loan");
        applications.offer("LOAN-2002: Priya car loan");
        applications.offer("LOAN-2003: Pankaj education loan");

        System.out.println("Processing in FIFO order:");
        while (!applications.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("  Processing: " + applications.poll());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Processing in FIFO order:
  Processing: LOAN-2001: Rajesh home loan
  Processing: LOAN-2002: Priya car loan
  Processing: LOAN-2003: Pankaj education loan
```

### How does a queue work internally?

ArrayDeque uses a resizable circular array. Two pointers, head and tail, track the front and rear of the queue. When you offer, the element is placed at the tail position and the tail advances. When you poll, the element at the head is returned and the head advances. Both pointers wrap around when they reach the end of the array, which is why it is called circular. When the array fills, it doubles in size, giving amortized O(1) cost.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Simplified circular queue to illustrate internals
public class CircularQueue {
    private String[] data;
    private int head = 0;
    private int tail = 0;
    private int size = 0;

    public CircularQueue(int capacity) {
        data = new String[capacity];
    }

    public void offer(String item) {
        if (size == data.length) throw new RuntimeException("Queue full");
        data[tail] = item;
        tail = (tail + 1) % data.length;
        size++;
    }

    public String poll() {
        if (size == 0) throw new RuntimeException("Queue empty");
        String item = data[head];
        data[head] = null;
        head = (head + 1) % data.length;
        size--;
        return item;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CircularQueue q = new CircularQueue(4);
        q.offer("SAV-1001");
        q.offer("SAV-1002");
        System.out.println("Polled: " + q.poll());
        q.offer("SAV-1003");
        System.out.println("Polled: " + q.poll());
        System.out.println("Polled: " + q.poll());
    }
}
```

```text
Polled: SAV-1001
Polled: SAV-1002
Polled: SAV-1003
```

### How do you create and use a queue in Java?

Java provides the `Queue` interface in `java.util`. The two main implementations are `ArrayDeque` and `LinkedList`. Prefer `ArrayDeque` for most use cases because it has better cache locality and no node-allocation overhead. Use `offer` and `poll` instead of `add` and `remove` because they return null or false on failure rather than throwing exceptions.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Queue;

public class QueueApiDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Preferred: ArrayDeque
        Queue<String> queue1 = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue1.offer("FT-101");
        queue1.offer("FT-102");
        System.out.println("ArrayDeque poll: " + queue1.poll());

        // Alternative: LinkedList
        Queue<String> queue2 = new LinkedList<>();
        queue2.offer("FT-201");
        queue2.offer("FT-202");
        System.out.println("LinkedList poll: " + queue2.poll());

        // Safe: poll returns null on empty queue
        Queue<String> empty = new ArrayDeque<>();
        System.out.println("Poll empty: " + empty.poll()); // null, no exception
    }
}
```

```text
ArrayDeque poll: FT-101
LinkedList poll: FT-201
Poll empty: null
```

### How is a queue different from a stack?

A queue is FIFO and a stack is LIFO. A queue serves the oldest element first, while a stack serves the newest. Both are linear structures with O(1) core operations, but they model opposite real-world patterns.

| Feature | Queue (FIFO) | Stack (LIFO) |
|---|---|---|
| Order | First In First Out | Last In First Out |
| Add | offer / enqueue (rear) | push (top) |
| Remove | poll / dequeue (front) | pop (top) |
| Use case | BFS, task scheduling, fairness | DFS, undo, expression eval |
| Java class | ArrayDeque (offer/poll) | ArrayDeque (push/pop) |

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.Queue;

public class QueueVsStack {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Queue<String> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.offer("First");
        queue.offer("Second");
        System.out.println("Queue serves: " + queue.poll()); // First

        Deque<String> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push("First");
        stack.push("Second");
        System.out.println("Stack serves: " + stack.pop());  // Second
    }
}
```

```text
Queue serves: First
Stack serves: Second
```

### What are the different types of queues?

Java offers several queue variants. A `PriorityQueue` serves elements by priority rather than arrival order. A `Deque` (double-ended queue) allows add and remove at both ends. A `BlockingQueue` blocks the calling thread when the queue is empty or full, which is ideal for producer-consumer patterns in banking transaction processing.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.PriorityQueue;
import java.util.Queue;

public class PriorityLoanQueue {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // PriorityQueue: smallest first by default
        Queue<Integer> urgency = new PriorityQueue<>();
        urgency.offer(3); // low priority
        urgency.offer(1); // high priority
        urgency.offer(2); // medium priority

        System.out.println("Serve by priority:");
        while (!urgency.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("  Priority " + urgency.poll());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Serve by priority:
  Priority 1
  Priority 2
  Priority 3
```

### What exceptions can occur?

Calling `remove()` or `element()` on an empty queue throws `NoSuchElementException`. That is why `poll()` and `peek()` are preferred: they return null instead. For a `BlockingQueue`, `put()` and `take()` block rather than throwing, which is safer for concurrent code.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.NoSuchElementException;
import java.util.Queue;

public class QueueExceptionDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Queue<String> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();

        // Wrong: remove() throws on empty
        try {
            queue.remove();
        } catch (NoSuchElementException e) {
            System.out.println("Caught: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }

        // Correct: poll() returns null on empty
        String result = queue.poll();
        System.out.println("Poll on empty: " + result);
    }
}
```

```text
Caught: NoSuchElementException
Poll on empty: null
```

### What about thread safety?

ArrayDeque and LinkedList are not thread-safe. For concurrent producer-consumer patterns, use `java.util.concurrent.LinkedBlockingQueue` or `ConcurrentLinkedQueue`. In a banking system where one thread receives transfer requests and another processes them, a `BlockingQueue` is the natural bridge.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.LinkedBlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue;

public class TransferProcessor {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        BlockingQueue<String> transfers = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(100);

        // Producer thread
        transfers.put("NEFT-3001: Rajesh to Priya 25000");
        transfers.put("NEFT-3002: Pankaj to Rajesh 50000");

        // Consumer thread
        System.out.println("Processing: " + transfers.take());
        System.out.println("Processing: " + transfers.take());
    }
}
```

```text
Processing: NEFT-3001: Rajesh to Priya 25000
Processing: NEFT-3002: Pankaj to Rajesh 50000
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Queues are the backbone of asynchronous systems. Distributed message brokers like Kafka, RabbitMQ, and Amazon SQS are durable queues that decouple producers from consumers. When the National Bank of India processes millions of salary-day transactions, it enqueues each request and worker threads dequeue them at a safe rate, smoothing out traffic spikes while preserving FIFO ordering.

The key architectural insight is that a queue decouples the speed of producers from the speed of consumers. The front end can accept requests as fast as they arrive and drop them into the queue, while the backend processes them steadily. This is the same pattern whether the queue is an in-memory ArrayDeque or a distributed Kafka topic with retention and replay.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Queue;

public class SalaryDayProcessor {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Queue<String> salaryTransfers = new ArrayDeque<>();

        // Simulate burst of salary-day requests
        for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
            String txn = "SAL-" + i + ": Credit salary to EMP-" + (1000 + i);
            salaryTransfers.offer(txn);
            System.out.println("Enqueued: " + txn);
        }

        System.out.println("\nProcessing at steady rate:");
        while (!salaryTransfers.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("  " + salaryTransfers.poll());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Enqueued: SAL-1: Credit salary to EMP-1001
Enqueued: SAL-2: Credit salary to EMP-1002
Enqueued: SAL-3: Credit salary to EMP-1003
Enqueued: SAL-4: Credit salary to EMP-1004
Enqueued: SAL-5: Credit salary to EMP-1005

Processing at steady rate:
  SAL-1: Credit salary to EMP-1001
  SAL-2: Credit salary to EMP-1002
  SAL-3: Credit salary to EMP-1003
  SAL-4: Credit salary to EMP-1004
  SAL-5: Credit salary to EMP-1005
```
